    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d280">

                <pb facs="0561" n="491" sameAs="frus:frus1883/pg491"/>

                <head>No. 280.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. West to Mr.
                    Frelinghuysen</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1883-01-15"><hi rendition="#italic">January</hi> 15, 1883</date>.
                        (Received January 16.)</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your note of the 28th of
                    November last, relative to the decree of the Venezuelan Government requiring
                    ships papers to be deposited with the port officers, I have the honor to inform
                    you that Harl Granville, in thanking you for this communication, has requested
                    me to state to you that, no report on the subject having been received . from
                    Her Majesty’s legation or consulate in Venezuela, instructions have been sent to
                    inquire into the matter, and that in the meanwhile Her Majesty’s minister will
                    act in concert with the representative of the United States and the
                    representatives of any other powers who maybe taking steps to prevent
                    inconvenience to vessels of their country which may frequent Venezuelan
                    ports.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.</p>
                <closer><signed>L. S. SACKVILLE WEST.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
